<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/foundational/how-to-learn-any-language">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Languages are not memorised — they are <strong>absorbed through meaningful contact</strong>, then
    cemented by use. You already know this from the inside: nobody drilled you the rules of your first
    language; you heard tens of thousands of hours of it being used to get real things done, and the
    system assembled itself. Second-language research has spent half a century turning that observation
    into a method, and the method that underlies nearly every successful learner has three load-bearing
    parts: massive <strong>comprehensible input</strong> (listening and reading just past your level —
    Stephen Krashen's "i+1"), <strong>spaced repetition</strong> for the words that must stick, and
    <strong>early speaking</strong> to force retrieval. Talent is mostly hours, arranged well. This
    lesson explains each part — why it works, what it looks like in practice — and then anchors it to
    the framework your syllabus actually uses: the CEFR levels, starting with A1, the "Breakthrough"
    level you can reach in weeks rather than years.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="vocabulary versus hours of practice — early words come fast; the plateau is where methods matter" widget="plotter" config={vocabCfg} />

  <p>
    The curve is honest and a little cruel. The first few hundred hours buy a huge share of everyday
    communication, because languages are wildly uneven in word value: a small core of high-frequency
    words does most of the work (the top 1,000 words typically cover the large majority of ordinary
    speech; the most frequent 2,000–3,000 push past 90% of everyday text and talk), then gains slow as
    you chase rarer words. Vocabulary researcher Paul Nation's estimates put <em>comfortable
    reading</em> — knowing 98% of the words on a page — at roughly 8,000–9,000 word families for a
    language like English. The gap between "can order dinner" and "can read a novel" is that plateau,
    and the plateau is where most learners quit — usually because they misread it as evidence they
    lack talent, when it is just the shape of the frequency distribution. Knowing <em>why</em> it
    exists keeps you going: the early words were frequent enough to meet thirty times a day; the late
    ones need deliberate hunting.
  </p>

  <h2>Comprehensible input: the engine</h2>
  <p>
    Krashen's <strong>input hypothesis</strong> (1982) claims acquisition happens when you understand
    messages slightly above your current level — <em>i + 1</em>, where <em>i</em> is what you already
    have. The mechanism is meaning-first: your brain is an excellent pattern extractor when it cares
    what the sentence is for. Watching a show you 80–90% understand, your attention is held by the
    story while the 10–20% unknown gets consistently surrounded by enough context to be absorbed —
    the unknown word sits inside known grammar, next to known words, doing an inferable job. This is
    why <em>graded readers</em> exist (real stories rewritten with a limited vocabulary), why children's
    content is a legitimate adult strategy, and why re-watching a familiar show with target-language
    audio works: you already know the plot, so every scrap of attention is free for the language. The
    practical corollary is the 80–90% rule: material that is <em>too</em> hard (you understand 40%)
    produces noise, frustration and no acquisition; material too easy produces comfort and no growth.
    Daily slightly-too-hard input — podcasts at walking pace, subtitled shows, graded news — is the
    single highest-yield habit in the field. And input must be <em>massive</em>: hours per week, not
    minutes, because the curve above is driven by exposure volume.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> studying grammar tables is not acquisition — grammar knowledge
  helps you notice patterns in input, but the patterns themselves are absorbed from contact, not
  memorised as rules. The test is classic: a learner can recite a tense rule perfectly and still fail
  to use it in conversation, because the conversational system runs on absorbed chunks, not conscious
  grammar. Study grammar to understand what you hear; acquire grammar by hearing it.</p>

  <h2>Spaced repetition: making words permanent</h2>
  <p>
    Input supplies words; <strong>spaced repetition (SRS)</strong> keeps them. The psychology is the
    forgetting curve you can read about on this atlas's psychology pages: memory decays exponentially,
    but each successful retrieval — and especially each retrieval that happens <em>just as the memory
    begins to fade</em> — flattens the decay dramatically. SRS software schedules exactly that: a card
    returns in 1 day, then 3, then 7, then 16, each interval stretched by your success. The result is
    remarkable efficiency: 15–20 minutes of daily cards can maintain thousands of words, because the
    system spends its time only on words about to be lost. Two refinements make SRS powerful rather
    than merely tidy. Learn <strong>frequency first</strong>: build or import decks of the most common
    1,000–2,000 words before anything else, because each of those words returns value every single
    day. And make cards from <em>your own</em> input — the word you met in last night's episode, with
    its sentence — because context is what the word actually lives in. Vocabulary without context is a
    phone book; with context it is speech.
  </p>

  <h2>Output: speaking before you're ready</h2>
  <p>
    The third engine is <strong>output</strong>, and its value is different from input's. Producing
    language <em>forces retrieval</em> — reaching for a word you have seen but never used, and
    successfully grabbing it, does more to fix that word than ten more sightings. Speaking also
    exposes gaps instantly: the moment you cannot say "I need to leave early tomorrow" is a precise
    map of what to learn next, which is why conversation partners and tutors function as accelerator,
    not merely practice. The standard advice — <em>speak before you feel ready</em> — follows from the
    psychology of errors: a mistake corrected in conversation is the fastest teacher available, and
    perfectionist delay protects your ego at the cost of the hours. Add deliberate <strong>shadowing</strong>
    (repeating audio a half-second behind the speaker) for pronunciation, because accent is a motor
    skill: your mouth needs the same hundreds of repetitions as your ears. Learn the <strong>script
    properly</strong> if there is one — Devanagari, kana, Hangul — using stroke order and mnemonics;
    Hangul's 1443 design is famously learnable in a weekend, and reading is the highest-bandwidth
    input channel you will ever own.
  </p>

  <h2>A1, concretely: what "Breakthrough" means</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>CEFR scale</strong> (Council of Europe, 2001) describes languages by what you can
    <em>do</em>, not by courses completed: A1 Breakthrough, A2 Waystage, B1 Threshold, B2 Vantage, C1
    Effective Proficiency, C2 Mastery. A1 is the syllabus unit this lesson anchors to, and its
    descriptors are worth reading as a shopping list, because they are startlingly achievable. The
    first: <em>understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic phrases aimed at the
    satisfaction of needs of a concrete type</em> — greetings, numbers, prices, "where is...?", "I
    would like...", ordering food, asking directions. The second: <em>introduce yourself and others,
    and ask and answer questions about personal details — where you live, people you know, things you
    have</em> — which is a memorisable-and-personalised mini-speech plus a bank of question forms
    ("What's your name? Where are you from? Do you have brothers or sisters?"). The third:
    <em>interact in a simple way provided the other person talks slowly and clearly and is prepared to
    help</em> — the crucial clause being the last one: A1 is a <em>cooperative</em> conversation, and
    learning the repair kit ("slower, please", "how do you say...?", "can you repeat that?") is
    itself A1 content, because it keeps the conversation alive past your vocabulary. Hours-wise,
    language schools typically budget on the order of 60–100 guided hours for A1 in a "close" language
    for an English speaker — far more for a distant one — but the honest unit is the curve's: input
    hours, arranged by the method above.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> adults are not worse language learners than children — they are
  different learners. Adults have worse accents more often (motor patterns fossilise) and less time,
  but they learn vocabulary and structure <em>faster</em>, can use literacy and metacognition, and can
  study deliberately. The "critical period" applies most sharply to native-like accent; the rest is
  hours. A1 in eight disciplined weeks is an ordinary adult outcome, not a gifted one.</p>

  <h2>Building the daily system</h2>
  <p>
    Method becomes progress only as a schedule, and the working shape is small-and-daily rather than
    long-and-weekly — the forgetting curve punishes gaps far more than it rewards marathons. A
    realistic A1 sprint on a close language: 20 minutes of SRS (frequency decks first), 30–45
    minutes of comprehensible input (a graded reader at breakfast, a subtitled episode at night),
    and 2–3 conversations per week with a tutor or exchange partner, plus <strong>shadowing</strong>
    five minutes a day for the motor skill of pronunciation. Two design rules make the system
    survive real life. First, <strong>anchor habits to existing ones</strong> — cards with morning
    coffee, input on the commute — because the habit, not the willpower, is the fragile part.
    Second, <strong>track inputs, not outcomes</strong>: hours listened, cards matured, sessions
    spoken. Outcomes (the plateau especially) lag weeks behind effort, and learners who watch only
    outcomes quit precisely when the curve is behaving normally; the input ledger is the honest
    scoreboard. The weekly review asks one question — <em>what was hard to say or understand this
    week?</em> — and turns each answer into next week's SRS cards, which is the loop that makes the
    system self-updating: your own failed conversations become your curriculum.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four refinements for the committed learner. <strong>Language distance sets the budget:</strong>
    the US Foreign Service Institute's category table is the standard yardstick — roughly 600–750
    class hours for professional proficiency in close languages (Spanish, French, Dutch), versus
    around 2,200 for the "super-hard" group (Japanese, Mandarin, Korean, Arabic) — not because those
    languages are harder in any absolute sense, but because less transfers from English; a Mandarin
    speaker finds Japanese vocabulary nearly free. Choose your expectations accordingly, and your
    first language strategically if you get to choose. <strong>Chunks beat words:</strong> much of
    fluent speech is stored multi-word sequences ("to be honest", "on the other hand", the entire
    phrase "could I have the bill, please?"), and learners who collect chunks speak faster and more
    idiomatically than those who build every sentence from vocabulary plus grammar — the finding
    behind teaching <em>collocations</em> explicitly. <strong>Tolerance of ambiguity is trainable:</strong>
    the A1-to-B1 stretch is full of moments where you catch only half the message; learners who can
    keep reading (and keep smiling) through partial understanding accumulate the input that resolves
    the ambiguity later, while those who need certainty stall. <strong>Identity is the hidden
    variable:</strong> research on motivation converged on the idea that learners persist when the
    language is part of who they are becoming — the imagined community they speak it with — which is
    why finding actual people (online tutors, language exchanges, a show's fandom) outperforms any
    app streak. The method beneath every successful learner, in one sentence: massive comprehensible
    input, spaced retrieval, brave early output, and enough contact with real speakers to keep the
    why alive — the curve will take care of the rest.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Krashen, Principles and Practice in Second Language Acquisition (1982); Nation, Learning Vocabulary in Another Language (2013); CEFR (Council of Europe, 2001); US Foreign Service Institute language category data.</p>
